5sed7D8S9Tv" displaytext="Strategies for Vocabulary Instruction with English Learners: Teaching Generatively " expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.63xAJGAN6uI.6gmE2kcVCgs" displaytext="Strategies for Vocabulary Instruction with English Language Learners: Reinforcing Disciplinary Language " expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.63xAJGAN6uI.69l7ev87qdl" displaytext="Strategies for Vocabulary Instruction with English Language Learners: Interactive Learning" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.63xAJGAN6uI.6nXtKrbCg5T" displaytext="Teaching English Language Learners Quick-Check" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.6VnXrL2qblR.6fW2JUgwvPo" displaytext="In Practice" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6VnXrL2qblR.5yr2QAOZyRy" displaytext="Video: Vocabulary Instruction Case Study " expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.6O52XnadHjJ" displaytext="Apply" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.5u5GEH7L9O9" displaytext="Reflecting on an English Language Learner " expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.5V2XzZJvxnx" displaytext="Collaborating with a Specialist" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.5uMW94RjtFJ" displaytext="Lesson Revision" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.6bMBZx0L5ld.6anlHbEJ1hA" displaytext="Reflect" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6bMBZx0L5ld.5cN1yA07wjF" displaytext="Reflection of Learning" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6bMBZx0L5ld.5XHj5UeHUpW" displaytext="Review Course Outcomes" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.6QuSGxA16uD.65SEJjbLsuw" displaytext="Look Forward" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6QuSGxA16uD.6UjQg7tzjGu" displaytext="Assignments" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6QuSGxA16uD.5tboUVrPLri" displaytext="In Preparation for the Next Session" expand="true" /></links></slidelink></links></outline><search><slidetext slideid="5XSzB7jZajQ.6MlS3J7yuGh" slidebank="false">vocabulary their way self-paced session 4: vocabulary instruction with english language learners tm </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6IjT3uCZGfL.60CRu1RJTZk" slidebank="false">welcome </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6IjT3uCZGfL.5jALJGZVNFv" slidebank="false">by the end of this blended course, you  will be able to do the following: understand vocabulary development and spelling-meaning connections. analyze vocabulary assessments to identify students’ needs and organize for instruction.  determine how best to instruct students using generative and word-specific strategies. select appropriate instructional strategies and activities to expand students’ academic vocabulary.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6IjT3uCZGfL.5wve6KIHWRu" slidebank="false">in your participant workbook, reflect on the top three ideas or strategies that resonated with you as you read the chapter. throughout this session, we will explore the ideas from the chapter in greater depth.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6ahnMkhpOck" slidebank="false">learn </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.5VONlhFdnmZ" slidebank="false">english language learners have a wide range of experiences with the english language. your vocabulary instruction will be much more successful with english language learners if you know what languages and background experiences students bring to their learning.  take a short quiz in your participant workbook to help you understand how a student’s language and literacy experiences can affect their vocabulary acquisition in english.  after you answer the question in your participant workbook, click the question mark icon to reveal the answer.    take the quiz </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6IPnyyjoAN2" slidebank="false">after you answer the question in your participant workbook, click the question mark button to reveal the answer.   if you notice that a student writes right to left, what indication of the home language could this be?  do all alphabets contain letters that represent a variety of sounds like the english alphabet? for example, c sounds different in the word cat than in the word cease. if a writing system uses characters, do the characters represent phonetic sounds, alphabetic letters, ideas, or a combination of these elements?   (helman et al. 2012, 12)  if you notice that a student writes right to left, what indication of the home language could this be?  in some languages, such as arabic, written language or script runs right to left. this is also true for the hebrew alphabet and n’ko script, which is used for several languages of africa.  (helman et al. 2012, 12)  do all alphabets contain letters that represent a variety of sounds like the english alphabet?  for example, c sounds different in the word cat than in the word cease.  no, in many languages, such as czech, each letter represents only one phonetic sound.  (helman et al. 2012, 12)  if a writing system uses characters, do the characters represent phonetic sounds, alphabetic letters, ideas, or a combination of these elements?  in many writing systems that use characters, such as chinese, there are tens of thousands of characters that represent both sound and meaning.  (helman et al. 2012, 12) </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.60kaPexwbEF" slidebank="false">click the video to begin.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6QnU1ukGbjD" slidebank="false">use your participant workbook to reflect upon the following question:  how can you get to know students individually and become aware of cultural differences among students? </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.5akyzG3j6lD" slidebank="false">sonia gretzky, a secondary science teacher, talks about the root struct and discusses how its meaning, “to build,” functions to contribute to the meaning of many words. she shares a complete list of struct words she obtained from www.onelook.com . . . “this one root occurs in well over 300 words in english! now let’s look at the root hydr- we don’t see it too often, do we? any ideas what it means?” if there’s no response, sonia may ask “if you have to hydrate someone after a long soccer practice on a hot day, what does that mean? she helps students understand that hydr usually means “water” or “fluid.” she then asks, “how many of you think this root will occur in more words than struct? fewer words?” sonia then shares the results of her onelook  search for *hydr*, and students are usually quite surprised to see that this root occurs in almost 900 words in english-more than three times as many words as struct.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.5jJtATaRbqt" slidebank="false">sonia continues: “wow! we’ve got a lot of curious-looking words in these lists! check them out: dehydrochlorinase, hydrocephalous, sterhydraulic-not exactly everyday words, are they? but somewhere inside of them, you know they’ve got something to do with ‘water’ or ‘fluid.’ and although you’re not going to run into the word dehydrochlorinase very often, you are going to run into the root hydr quite a bit in science.    (templeton et al. 2010, 105)  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6BIjxw5k7P7" slidebank="false">click the video to begin.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6PkQyqrFBB0" slidebank="false">in your participant workbook, list the “secrets” of the english language that need to be revealed to english language learners.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.5xISUt1b73d" slidebank="false">mr. bell, a middle school social studies teacher, has been working hard to get to know the language and literacy backgrounds of his english language learners. he has also started to incorporate a focus on teaching generatively by calling to attention meaningful word parts when introducing new vocabulary.  soo is a new student in mr. bell’s class. through conversation with soo and observing soo’s conversations with fellow students, mr. bell has noticed how well soo is able to communicate. mr. bell has found out all about soo’s family and how they came to america. he has learned soo’s interests and goals for the future. mr. bell is surprised that soo is part of the english language program.  students recently took an exam following a unit on westward expansion. soo did very poorly on the exam, and mr. bell is puzzled. he seemed engaged in class discussions and never expressed that there was anything he didn’t understand.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.5ysLPcpNzxX" slidebank="false">in your participant workbook, reflect on some possible reasons for soo’s poor performance on the exam.  when you are finished, click the video to listen to language expert, jim cummins, phd, discuss some factors that could explain soo’s performance on the exam.  click the video to begin.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.69l7ev87qdl" slidebank="false">click the video to begin.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6UeI2JaKNTJ" slidebank="false">reflect on your own teaching. choose one class that you teach and think about the last week of instruction. how many times have students had the opportunity to talk to one another to facilitate their learning? how might you incorporate more opportunities for students to interact?  record your thoughts in your participant workbook.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6nXtKrbCg5T" slidebank="false">time for a quick-check!  respond to the teaching english language learners quick-check questions in your participant workbook.  how is academic or disciplinary language different than conversational language?  how does a student’s home language impact his or her acquisition of english?  why is generative vocabulary instruction particularly useful for teaching english language learners?  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VnXrL2qblR.6fW2JUgwvPo" slidebank="false">in practice </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VnXrL2qblR.5yr2QAOZyRy" slidebank="false">you have been meeting with a few colleagues on a weekly basis to discuss vocabulary instruction. you bounce ideas off one another and share challenges, success stories, and resources. you decide to partner up and observe one another so that you can receive feedback about your vocabulary instruction. you are paired with ms. pezzuto, a middle school language arts teacher.  click the video to begin.  as you observe her lessons, take notes in your participant workbook. then, prepare an e-mail to her highlighting your feedback on her lesson.  click play to watch ms. pezzuto’s lesson when you are ready.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.6O52XnadHjJ" slidebank="false">apply </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.5u5GEH7L9O9" slidebank="false">now it is time to apply what you’ve learned. for the next exercise, you will need to select one english language learner you currently teach or have taught in the past. this student will be the focus of your reflection.  using your participant workbook, you will answer a series of questions about the student to the best of your ability. when you are finished, you will rate your knowledge of the student’s language and literacy background.  finally, you will use the questionnaire as a catalyst to create a list of five things you want to know about all of your english language learners.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.66vELIp0SbY" slidebank="false">in your participant workbook, create a list of talking points or discussion questions that you will use when you set up your meeting with an english language learner teacher or specialist.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.5uMW94RjtFJ" slidebank="false">you brought a sample vocabulary  lesson that you have taught to the session. now that you have learned considerations for english language learners, you will evaluate your lesson using a rubric in the participant workbook. if your lesson doesn’t address strategies for english language  learners, think about which  components could be revised to  better meet the needs of english language learners.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6bMBZx0L5ld.6anlHbEJ1hA" slidebank="false">reflect </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6bMBZx0L5ld.5cN1yA07wjF" slidebank="false">in your participant workbook, reflect on vocabulary instruction with english language learners by creating a concise summary of what you learned that could be shared with colleagues by e-mail or at a staff meeting. if you would like, consider using the stem “there are a few important things to keep in mind as you plan vocabulary instruction with english language learners…”   record any questions that you still have in the still wondering section of your participant workbook. you will have a chance to ask these questions to the facilitator in the live session, later on in the course. ? ? ? </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6bMBZx0L5ld.5XHj5UeHUpW" slidebank="false">by the end of this blended course, you  will be able to do the following: understand vocabulary development and  spelling-meaning connections. analyze vocabulary assessments to identify  students’ needs and organize for instruction.  determine how best to instruct students using generative and word-specific strategies. select appropriate instructional strategies  and activities to expand students’ academic vocabulary.  today, we focused primarily on this outcome.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6QuSGxA16uD.65SEJjbLsuw" slidebank="false">look forward </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6QuSGxA16uD.6UjQg7tzjGu" slidebank="false">to apply what you have learned and engage in a professional discussion, respond to the discussion questions on the discussion board. remember to respond to at least two participants’ posts in addition to posting your own response to each question. also, spend time in the next week learning about how your school district supports english language learners. find out about the resources available to students.  lastly, make contact with an english language learner teacher or specialist from your district and set up a time to meet with them. use the questions or discussion points that you created in this session to guide the conversation.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6QuSGxA16uD.5tboUVrPLri" slidebank="false">in preparation for the next session, do the following: read chapters 3 and 4 of vocabulary their way.  </slidetext></search></nav_data><resource_data description="&lt;p align='left'&gt;&lt;font face='Arial' size='11' color='#444444'&gt;Here are some useful links and documents:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" /><transcript_data /><glossary_data /></bwFrame>